.TH QRQ 1 "August 2026" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
qrq \- High speed Morse telegraphy trainer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qrq 
.RB [ --version ]

.B qrqscore
[OPTION]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qrq
is an open source Morse code 
trainer for various platforms and operating
systems, similar to the classic DOS
version of Rufz by DL4MM.

It's not intended for learning telegraphy (have a look at
http://radio.linux.org.au/ for CW learning software), but to improve the
ability to copy callsigns at high speeds, as needed for example for contesting. 

After starting the software and setting up the parameters, the program sends
50 callsigns or words by default. When a callsign is copied correctly, the
speed increases by the configured up step; otherwise it decreases by the
configured down step. A character-speed floor enables Farnsworth spacing when
the overall speed falls below it. Points depend on speed and copy accuracy.

F5 opens the settings selector. Use Up/Down to select a setting and
Left/Right to change it. Tab or Page Down selects the next general, audio, or
call-filter page; Shift-Tab or Page Up selects the previous page. Enter edits
a selected text setting. F6 repeats the current item once by default; the
optional f6 setting allows additional resends. During an attempt F7 replays the
previous item; outside an attempt it shows local session history. F10 aborts
the attempt or exits the program.

The call-filter page can generate zero-padded three-, four-, or five-digit
contest serial exchanges in place of a callsign database. These serial-copy
sessions are training-only and are not comparable toplist entries.

The W4GNS portable-call generator replaces eligible base calls with /P, /M,
and /MM variants. It overrides the ordinary portable-suffix filter and is a
training-only, non-comparable session.

The W4GNS custom-item generator accepts a comma-separated list such as
CQ,DE,5NN,? and replaces the selected database and other generators. Items
are normalized to uppercase, may include CW punctuation and spaces, and are
limited to 28 characters. Custom-item sessions are training-only and
non-comparable.

W4GNS sustained-copy goals combine a target speed with a duration. The goal
passes only when the operator remains at or above the configured speed until
the duration expires; QRQ reports a concise result and next-step recommendation.
Sustained goals are training-only and are not comparable toplist entries.

Only completed, ordinary 50-call sessions are added to the comparable local
toplist. Fixed-speed, unlimited-repeat, non-50-call, adaptive, missed-item
review, serial-copy, portable-variant, custom-item, sustained-goal, and
seeded sessions remain in history but are toplist-ineligible.
Aborted sessions are excluded, and an optional accuracy goal must be met.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --version
Print the QRQ version to the console and exit.

A scoreboard/toplist exists on the author's website, at
https://fkurz.net/ham/qrqtop.php. The local toplist file can be synchronized
with the online toplist with 
.B qrqscore -d
and you can upload your own top scores by invoking
.B qrqscore -u.

.SH FILES
.I qrqrc
.RS
Default configuration file
.RE
.I callbase.qcb
.RS
Database of amateur radio callsigns used by qrq. Other files may be loaded.
.RE
.I morserunner.qcb
.RS
W4GNS-integrated Super Check Partial contest callsign snapshot (50,029 calls).
.RE
.I toplist
.RS
Default toplist file
.RE
.I toplist.history.csv
.RS
Versioned local session history, including training sessions
.RE
.I Summary/
.RS
Detailed, uniquely named summaries of attempts
.RE

.B qrq
is looking for these files in the current directory, in ~/.qrq/ or under
PREFIX/share/qrq/ (in that order). PREFIX defaults to /usr and is selected at
build time. If installed resources are used, ~/.qrq/ is created for the user's
toplist, history, summaries, and configuration.
